{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-110423",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Since the partial derivatives of $F(x,y)$ are defined, the joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂x∂y}F(x,y).\n$$\n\nFor $x\\ge 0$ and $y\\ge 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂x∂y}F(x,y) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1-{e}^{-x})(1-{e}^{-y})) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}((1-{e}^{-x})({e}^{-y})) \\\\  & ={e}^{-x-y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOtherwise, $f(x,y)=0.$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-x-y}, & x\\ge 0,\\,y\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
